DIAGNOSTIC patch, not a fix - kept for reproducibility of the finding
below, not because it should ship. See kernel/patches/0008-...patch's
status note for the full trail up to this point (cluster-level
domain-idle-state disable alone: real, partial improvement, but a
panic dump caught multiple CPUs' idle tasks with a real NMI backtrace
literally inside cpu_suspend()/psci_cpu_suspend_enter() - the actual
PSCI CPU_SUSPEND firmware call).

This patch additionally strips domain-idle-states from all 8 cpu_pdN
per-CPU power domains, so no CPU ever calls into PSCI CPU_SUSPEND at
all - only the generic `cpu_do_idle` (plain WFI, no firmware SMC).
Purpose: find out whether the freeze persists with PSCI's CPU_SUSPEND
firmware call completely out of the picture.

RESULT (2026-09-21, tested with panic_on_rcu_stall/softlockup_panic/
hung_task_panic all =1 and CONFIG_PSTORE_COMPRESS off, so this is a
clean, readable panic-time pstore capture, not an inference from a
partial dump): the freeze still happens, and this is now airtight
evidence that PSCI CPU_SUSPEND is not required for it. `rcu_preempt`
detected a real stall; the kernel correctly tried to panic and shut
every CPU down via NMI as a last resort - and:

    SMP: stopping secondary CPUs
    SMP: retry stop with NMI for CPUs 1,3-5
    SMP: failed to stop secondary CPUs 1,3-5

Multiple CPUs could not be stopped even by the kernel's own forced
NMI shutdown, with every CPU idle state Linux controls disabled. This
points below cpuidle/PSCI entirely - most likely a genuine
interconnect/NoC bus-fabric stall under heavy DDR bandwidth pressure
(CPUs stay architecturally alive but block forever on an ordinary
load/store to anything sharing that fabric), matching the rationale
already written into kernel/config/gts7l.fragment's own
CONFIG_QCOM_ICC_BWMON comment from before this investigation. Next
step: check whether QCOM_ICC_BWMON is actually active and what
bandwidth votes are in place during the freeze - see
docs/load-test-modules.md (2026-09-21) for the full writeup.

Left un-reverted for now while that follow-up is in progress; revert
before considering anything here for a real fix (it gives up every
real low-power CPU idle state device-wide, which is a large,
unnecessary battery cost with no benefit - it was purely diagnostic).

